{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"Verified in SCOPe release 2.08 (the stable release; I did not query the later 2.08-2026-08-16 periodic update): all three are families within superfamily c.2.1, 'NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains' (sunid 51735), fold c.2, class c (alpha/beta). The three are sibling families, not parent and child: c.2.1.1 'Alcohol dehydrogenase-like, C-terminal domain' (sunid 51736), c.2.1.8 'CoA-binding domain' (sunid 51900), and c.2.1.9 'Potassium channel NAD-binding domain' (sunid 63944). Their family-level homology is therefore not asserted to be closer than the superfamily; SCOPe's homology record for c.2.1 has homology_scope 'sf', which means SCOPe asserts common ancestry at the superfamily level for the Rossmann-fold members, and each family record has homology_scope 'fa'. No warnings or heterogeneity flags were returned for any of the four nodes. The c.2.1.1 annotation says 'N-terminal all-beta domain defines family', so its family placement rests on a feature I did not check against a domain sequence or boundary. The c.2.1.9 record carries an automated Pfam mapping (PF02254).

Interpretation: a biologist should read this as one ancient dinucleotide-binding Rossmann-type module that diverged into several functionally distinct families (alcohol-dehydrogenase-like C-terminal, CoA-binding, and potassium-channel NAD-binding). The shared superfamily supports probable common ancestry of the Rossmann-type core, not a shared substrate, cofactor role, or catalytic activity. The family names are the only functional labels I retrieved; they should not be turned into a claim that the three families bind the same ligand or perform the same reaction. Divergence within the superfamily is the expected reading, and SCOPe does not treat the three families as analogous or convergent.

Limits: I did not retrieve domain SIDs, endpoints, sequences, BLAST or FATCAT hits, or structures for these families, so this answer makes no claim about specific proteins, boundaries, or residue-level conservation. Natural-language name searches for the three families returned no hits; I found them by browsing the children of c.2.1 instead. Any transfer of function, ligand, or structural template from one family to another needs family-specific experimental or structural evidence that SCOPe does not provide here.","evidence_used":["skill: scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info (release 2.08)","mcp__scope__search_scop (token/auto name searches, empty results)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sunid 63944 with parents; sccs c.2.1 with parents; sunid 51735, 51736, 51900, 63944 with annotations and homology)","mcp__scope__list_scop_children (sccs c.2.1, limit 50)","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/children?limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology"]}]}
